/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 177 0 R
/E (Dear participants of the IFCRS Early Career Researcher Symposium,)
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [0]
>>
endobj
254 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 177 0 R
/E (We meet at a time when the boundary between living coral reefs and the fossil record feels uncomfortably thin. Modern reefs are undergoing rapid and profound environmental change, while palaeontology reminds us that reef ecosystems have repeatedly experienced crises, reorganizations, and recoveries throughout Earth history. Understanding this long perspective is therefore no longer only a matter of reconstructing the past. It is increasingly relevant to understanding the present and considering what the future of reefs may look like.)
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
255 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 177 0 R
/E <54686520636F6E747269627574696F6E7320746F2074686973207965617290732073796D706F7369756D20696C6C7573747261746520706172746963756C61726C792077656C6C20686F77206D616E7920646966666572656E74206B696E6473206F662065766964656E636520617265206E656564656420746F2061646472657373207468657365207175657374696F6E732E>
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
256 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 177 0 R
/E (Some presentations explore how reefs have responded to environmental change on very different timescales. Mike Hynes uses Holocene reef cores from the Spermonde Archipelago to examine how reefs and low-lying islands adjusted to sea-level rise and environmental stress. Matthew Rahamut investigates how late Pleistocene Jamaican reefs maintained diverse coral communities under contrasting carbonate and siliciclastic regimes, while Katherine Faulkner combines the fossil history of the Coral Triangle with observations of modern bleaching to understand why some reef communities may be more resilient than others.)
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
257 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 177 0 R
/E (Other studies ask where corals persist when conditions become unfavorable. Danijela Dimitrijevic examines the deep-time history of reef refugia, including deeper, turbid and higher-latitude environments, reminding us that survival through a crisis and recovery of a complete reef ecosystem are very different processes. Samantha Hartzell approaches resilience from another direction, asking whether skeletal traits inherited from ancient scleractinian lineages persist in modern solitary and deep-water corals and whether these long-lived evolutionary strategies may be relevant to their survival today.)
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
258 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 177 0 R
/E (Several contributions also demonstrate how strongly our reconstruction of the past depends on the quality and interpretation of the geological record itself. Charlotte Spruzen shows that preservational processes may preferentially erase thrombolitic textures and thereby distort our perception of early microbial ecosystems. Jonathon Leonard uses the distribution of fossil Scleractinia to test competing reconstructions of Mesozoic and Cenozoic temperatures, illustrating that biological distributions can provide an important independent constraint on geochemical and model-derived estimates.  )
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
259 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 177 0 R
/T <FEFF0043004F004E00540049004E00550045004400200049004E0020004E00450058005400200050004100470045002027F60020>
/E <FEFF0043004F004E00540049004E00550045004400200049004E0020004E00450058005400200050004100470045002027F60020>
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
260 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 178 0 R
/E (Ajani Bissick explores how environmental conditions controlled microbialite architecture on an Ediacaran carbonate platform, while Zaid Qureshi combines detailed morphology with computational fluid-dynamic and structural modelling to test functional interpretations of Cambrian archaeocyaths.)
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [0]
>>
endobj
