/S /P
/P 178 0 R
/E (There is another reason why I think this emphasis on observation, evidence and continuity of knowledge is particularly important today. We are entering an era in which artificial intelligence can assist us with literature searches, data processing, modelling, image analysis and even scientific writing. These tools will undoubtedly become increasingly useful. But they also make it more important than ever that science remains grounded in reliable observations, careful measurements, reproducible data and genuine expertise.)
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
263 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 178 0 R
/E (Scientific interpretation is not produced simply by assembling information. It grows from years of looking at specimens, sections, outcrops and datasets; from making measurements ourselves; from recognizing when something does not fit expectations; and from developing the intuition to distinguish an interesting signal from an artefact. This kind of scientific intuition cannot be generated if we progressively outsource the process of thinking itself. AI may help us work more efficiently, but it should not replace the intellectual experience through which a researcher learns how to ask good questions and how to judge whether an answer is credible. )
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
264 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 178 0 R
/E (This is particularly important for early-career researchers. Your greatest scientific asset will not be the ability to obtain an instant answer, but the ability to recognize when an answer is wrong, incomplete or genuinely surprising. That ability develops through direct engagement with evidence and through discussions with other scientists.)
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
265 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 178 0 R
/T <FEFF0043004F004E00540049004E00550045004400200049004E0020004E00450058005400200050004100470045002027F6>
/E <FEFF0043004F004E00540049004E00550045004400200049004E0020004E00450058005400200050004100470045002027F6>
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
266 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 179 0 R
/E (This is exactly why meetings such as the IFCRS Early Career Researcher Symposium matter. They allow us to compare approaches, question interpretations and connect researchers studying modern and fossil reef ecosystems across very different temporal and spatial scales. They also help maintain the continuity of knowledge between generations of scientists.)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [0]
>>
endobj
267 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 179 0 R
/E (One of the primary goals of IFCRS is to support early-career researchers working on corals, reefs and reef-related ecosystems and to encourage them to become active members of our scientific community. We hope that many of the researchers presenting here will continue to develop these ideas, collaborations and friendships, and that we will meet again at future IFCRS events, including the 15th International Symposium of Fossil Cnidaria and Reefs in Toulouse in 2027.)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
268 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 179 0 R
/E (I would like to extend my sincere thanks to the organizers of this event Alison Cribb, Amanda Godbold, Claire Williams and Jood Al Aswad for the time and effort they have put into its organization. )
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
269 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 179 0 R
/E (I wish you an inspiring symposium, stimulating discussions, and above all the confidence to trust your observations, your data and your developing scientific intuition.)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
