399 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 186 0 R
/E (Coral reefs and coastal oceans are vital global "hubs," and provide critical economic and health benefits to billions of people. Yet, these environments are on the front lines of degradation and collapse due to accelerating climate change. Our group's research program is designed to meet this challenge by leveraging natural physical and biological interactions to investigate biogeochemical "fingerprints" of ecosystem health. We focus on three core areas: \(1\) developing novel geochemical tools for the early detection of environmental change; \(2\) using natural archives like corals, speleothems, and bivalves, to unravel historical and ancient records of environmental shifts; and \(3\) identifying mitigation strategies that best preserve biodiversity and ecosystem services. Crucially, we also engage deeply with coastal communities to translate our science into action. By providing environmental data for policymaking, education, and cultural preservation, we are fundamentally driven to use our science as a resource to catalyze environmental stewardship and resilience for coastal and historically marginalized populations.)
/Pg 23 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
