523 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 198 0 R
/E (What are the ancestral traits of order Scleractinia, and are these traits present in modern corals? Long-term stability of a basal morphology may indicate resilience within the scleractinian lineage. Taxonomists first attempted to trace the evolutionary relationships of fossil and modern corals utilizing skeletal morphology, though widespread agreement on these relationships was not reached until the advent of molecular methods. Molecular clock studies indicate that order Scleractinia originated in the Middle Ordovician. Scleractinia persisted in mostly soft-bodied forms which failed to fossilize throughout the Paleozoic, after which skeletonized forms appear in the Middle Triassic and persist to the present day. Skeletal morphologies could not determine evolutionary relationships among scleractinian taxa, but when combined with a molecular phylogeny as a framework, morphology can provide important insights.)
/Pg 35 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
524 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 198 0 R
/E (Skeletal macromorphologies were compiled for 31 fossil and 19 extant solitary scleractinian families, and 236 fossil and 104 modern solitary scleractinian genera. Parsimony-based trait node analysis was performed using 69 skeletal traits present in scleractinian families mapped to a published molecular phylogeny. Results indicate the basal scleractinian morphology included a trochoid or discoid corallum, laminar septa, septal teeth, ornamented lateral flanks, axial structure and costae. This collection of traits, the Persistent Ancestral Morphology, is retained by modern solitary corals across clades Vacatina and Refertina, though more members of Vacatina retain all basal features. Corals with the Persistent Ancestral Morphology inhabit a wide range of water depths and latitudes, including shelf, slope, and bathyal environments. Direct analysis of occurrence data for corals with the Persistent Ancestral Morphology is complicated by historical undersampling of solitary and deep-water corals, though machine-learning models indicate they are at risk for regional rapid biodiversity decline. Increased attention to solitary, deep water corals will be crucial to determining the trajectory of coral biodiversity in modern oceans.)
/Pg 35 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
