endobj
534 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 200 0 R
/E (The Caribbean has experienced numerous coral diseases and mortality events, including Acropora die-offs in the 1970s and 1980s and unprecedented, repeated mass bleaching in recent years. Stony Coral Tissue Loss Disease \(SCTLD\) is one of the latest threats, affecting many coral species, including the slow-growing massive corals that contribute to the reef framework. Do SCTLD's effects extend beyond community change to the reef and to what these reefs ultimately leave in the geological record? While this threat prompted new monitoring, coordination, funding, and political attention, that support is finite. )
/Pg 37 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
535 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 200 0 R
/E (We approach this as a memory problem. Fewer and fewer scientists and divers remain who remember what the Caribbean Acropora fields looked like. Ecologically, shifting baselines may lead scientists, divers, and other stakeholders to accept more degraded reefs as normal. Institutionally, similar loss can occur when long-term datasets lapse, response networks disband, and lessons from one disaster or outbreak are not carried forward into the next. Across the Caribbean, relatively few people are trained and funded to maintain long-term monitoring records, so institutional memory can depend on a small number of individuals and short-term projects. Drawing on policy and practitioner insights from SCTLD response efforts, we ask which crisis-era practices may be the most vulnerable to loss, and which institutional, financial, and behavioral incentives may drive that loss. How, then, might incentives be redesigned to build adaptive management into routine governance, sustain the people, datasets, and coordination that hold long-term memory? )
/Pg 37 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
536 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 200 0 R
/E (Using comparative policy analysis informed by conservation finance, we propose an incentive-based framework that rewards data continuity, learning, and prevention. Coral scientists, including those working on historical and geological records, have a stake in whether institutional memory persists. SCTLD will not be the last outbreak, so the question is what we carry into the next one. )
/Pg 37 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
