548 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 203 0 R
/T (Coral community composition and diversity of late Pleistocene reefs under contrasting sediment regimes \(Jamaica\))
/E (Coral community composition and diversity of late Pleistocene reefs under contrasting sediment regimes \(Jamaica\))
/Pg 40 0 R
/K [0]
>>
endobj
549 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 203 0 R
/E (The late Pleistocene coral reefs of Jamaica serve as a natural laboratory for studying long-term ecological patterns in growth and biodiversity. They may also serve as an ecological baseline and standard for comparing anthropogenic effects on modern reef ecosystems. This study compares the coral biodiversity of two approximately coeval reefs deposited during the Last Interglacial Warm Period- the Falmouth Formation and the Port Morant Formation. The formations preserve both crest and backreef-to-lagoonal facies under contrasting sediment regimes. The Falmouth Fm. is a predominantly carbonate system that grew near a rocky limestone shoreline, whereas the Port Morant Fm. reefs grew with siliciclastic sediment at the edge of a delta front.)
/Pg 40 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
550 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 203 0 R
/E (Coral assemblages at each location were quantified using point intercept transects, and photo-quadrats were point-counted in CPCe Software. Community structure was evaluated using taxonomic abundance, species richness and diversity metrics, and multivariate analyses. Preliminary analyses indicate differences in community structure between the two formations and reef zones. The backreef showed greater diversity than the crest assemblage in both ecosystems, with the crest characterised by the dominance of framework-building taxa. Importantly, the siliciclastic-influenced Port Morant Fm. reefs retain diverse coral assemblages despite their differences in taxonomic composition.)
/Pg 40 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
551 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 203 0 R
/E (The results suggest that siliciclastic input did not preclude reef development during the Last Interglacial, but instead shaped the taxonomic organisation of coral communities. The comparison highlights the capacity of late Pleistocene Caribbean reefs to maintain diverse assemblages across markedly different sedimentary regimes.)
/Pg 40 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
552 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /Figure
/P 203 0 R
/Pg 40 0 R
/A [875 0 R 0]
/K [4]
>>
