556 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 204 0 R
/T (Preservational variation of a middle Tonian microbialite reef and implications for the under-recognition of ancient thrombolites)
/E (Preservational variation of a middle Tonian microbialite reef and implications for the under-recognition of ancient thrombolites)
/Pg 41 0 R
/K [0]
>>
endobj
557 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 204 0 R
/E (The Precambrian reef record is dominated by laminated microbially-induced precipitates called stromatolites. Thrombolites, which are unlaminated microbialites defined by a clotted mesoscale texture, are comparatively sparsely recognized and poorly understood. To investigate the significance of this discrepancy, we studied a middle Tonian reef in northwestern Canada that contains both stromatolites and thrombolites at variable stages of preservation. Our petrographic and geochemical data show that while stromatolitic lamination is retained at the mesoscale and microscale, thrombolite texture is overprinted by replacement phases in samples with similar paragenetic histories and geochemical profiles. As a result, thrombolitic rocks are typically unrecognizable as biogenic in outcrop without stratigraphic context and a complete preservational sequence as a reference. A comprehensive compilation of thrombolite occurrences from the Archean to the Holocene shows sparse documented thrombolites until the late Ediacaran Period, when the number and proportion of calcite thrombolites significantly increases. We suggest extensive dolomitization and silicification of ancient thrombolites due to their higher primary porosity caused their widespread textural obliteration. In contrast, stromatolites tend to retain lamination due to their lower initial porosity and stronger primary textural grain. This process occurred throughout the sedimentary record, but the widespread preservation of calcite from the late Ediacaran Period onwards enhanced the preservation of thrombolite textures. Together, our data from the Reefal assemblage and the microbialite compilation imply that the documented sedimentological record of ancient microbial life is strongly biased towards stromatolites, with implications for the completeness of the microbial evolutionary record and the deep-time carbon cycle.)
/Pg 41 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
